> Default Min-Heap (Natural Ordering)
  > Smallest element first. Works with any Comparable type (Integer, String, LocalDate, etc.).
    1* PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();
          pq.add(30); pq.add(10); pq.add(20);
          // pq.poll() → 10, then 20, then 30

     2* Max-Heap (Reverse Ordering)
          Largest element first. Built using Collections.reverseOrder().
          
          
     Type 2: Max-Heap (Reverse Ordering)     
          PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(Collections.reverseOrder());
          pq.add(30); pq.add(10); pq.add(20);
          // pq.poll() → 30, then 20, then 10
          Type 3: Custom Comparator PriorityQueue
          You define the priority rule yourself — e.g. by marks descending, by age ascending, by urgency.
          
     Type 3: Custom Comparator PriorityQueue 
          PriorityQueue<Student> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(
              (a, b) -> Integer.compare(b.marks, a.marks)   // higher marks = higher priority
          );
          Type 4: PriorityQueue from an Existing Collection (Heapify)
          Build a heap instantly from a List or any Collection — O(n) heapify instead of n × O(log n) inserts.
          
  Type 4: PriorityQueue from an Existing Collection (Heapify)
          List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(50, 20, 40, 10, 30);
          PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(list);
          Type 5: PriorityQueue with Initial Capacity
          Pre-size the internal array to avoid resizing — a micro-optimization for known workloads.
          
    Type 5: PriorityQueue with Initial Capacity     
          PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(100);       // capacity 100
          PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(100, Collections.reverseOrder()); // both
          Type 6: Thread-Safe Variant — PriorityBlockingQueue
          PriorityQueue is not thread-safe. For concurrent use, wrap or switch to PriorityBlockingQueue.
          
    
Type 6: Thread-Safe Variant — PriorityBlockingQueue     
          BlockingQueue<Task> pq = new PriorityBlockingQueue<>();
          Type 7: PriorityQueue of Non-Comparable Objects
          Requires either a Comparator in the constructor or the class must implement Comparable.
 Type 7: PriorityQueue of Non-Comparable Objects        
          class Task implements Comparable<Task> {
              int priority;
              public int compareTo(Task other) {
                  return Integer.compare(this.priority, other.priority);
              }
          }
          PriorityQueue<Task> pq = new PriorityQueue<>();   // uses compareTo()

